1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,676
Bob built this app, IBM's own demo, in English only.

2
00:00:04,676 --> 00:00:06,755
Watch Bob make it world ready.

3
00:00:06,755 --> 00:00:08,110
Here it is in English.

4
00:00:08,110 --> 00:00:09,755
Now, pick Arabic.

5
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:12,432
This is WorldReady.

6
00:00:12,432 --> 00:00:16,919
IBM Bob takes an app global, proves it, and keeps it that way.

7
00:00:16,919 --> 00:00:19,893
Translating text is the easy part.

8
00:00:19,893 --> 00:00:27,103
The real work is glued plurals, hard-coded prices, left-to-right layout, and English creeping back in.

9
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:31,364
On the left is IBM's English-only app.

10
00:00:31,364 --> 00:00:35,509
The scan outlines forty-seven hard-coded strings in red.

11
00:00:35,509 --> 00:00:37,601
On the right is WorldReady.

12
00:00:37,601 --> 00:00:38,443
Zero.

13
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:41,967
In Arabic, the whole app flips.

14
00:00:41,967 --> 00:00:43,757
Navigation is mirrored.

15
00:00:43,757 --> 00:00:50,060
On the flights page, dates and prices follow the Arabic locale, and planet names are translated.

16
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:51,520
French is clean too.

17
00:00:52,450 --> 00:00:55,321
Here is what machine translation can't do.

18
00:00:55,321 --> 00:01:02,886
Arabic has six plural forms: zero, one, two, a few, many, and other.

19
00:01:02,886 --> 00:01:06,057
All six are rendered from the real shipped bundle.

20
00:01:06,057 --> 00:01:08,334
One seats left never happens.

21
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:13,907
It holds up on a phone too, right to left, at three hundred ninety pixels.

22
00:01:14,810 --> 00:01:19,128
Translations start as machine drafts, so a human reviews them.

23
00:01:19,128 --> 00:01:23,786
Our reviewer changed seven cells in the translator spreadsheet.

24
00:01:23,786 --> 00:01:27,023
Bob read the sheet back and applied all seven.

25
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:37,426
The proof travels with the app: an evidence drawer with the trap scores, the held-out key's fingerprint, the translator sheet, and every Bob session.

26
00:01:38,350 --> 00:01:39,405
How was it built?

27
00:01:39,405 --> 00:01:41,997
The product work was done in IBM Bob.

28
00:01:41,997 --> 00:01:45,918
Bob planned the retrofit from a glossary and a style guide.

29
00:01:45,918 --> 00:01:49,589
It wrote a custom mode, a skill, and a commit hook.

30
00:01:49,589 --> 00:01:57,313
Then five sub-agents ran in parallel and moved three hundred forty-eight strings into keys, in about twelve minutes.

31
00:01:57,313 --> 00:02:04,418
Bob translated to French and Arabic, fixed the layout for right to left, and built this viewer.

32
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:06,345
Now the gate.

33
00:02:06,345 --> 00:02:09,608
Bob adds a hard-coded badge and tries to commit.

34
00:02:09,608 --> 00:02:10,858
The hook blocks it.

35
00:02:10,858 --> 00:02:14,872
Bob moves the text into a key, and the commit goes through.

36
00:02:14,872 --> 00:02:20,924
On GitHub, a pull request with hard-coded English goes red, and the fix turns it green.

37
00:02:21,830 --> 00:02:26,280
What you saw: three hundred five hard-coded strings, down to zero.

38
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:32,740
Twenty-four of thirty traps fixed, scored against a held-out key that was fingerprinted before Bob ran.

39
00:02:32,740 --> 00:02:35,293
Zero characters of English changed.

40
00:02:35,293 --> 00:02:36,924
One language to three.

41
00:02:36,924 --> 00:02:41,806
Nineteen Bob tasks, and nineteen point four of forty Bob coins.

42
00:02:41,806 --> 00:02:44,635
Try it yourself, and pick Arabic.
